/** \addtogroup area_geometry */
/** \defgroup theory_geometry Theory of geometry
 \ingroup area_geometry

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/geometry` solve two problems on a finite
 set of points of the plane: the convex hull, which section 16.1 of the book
 treats as an example of backtracking, and the closest pair, which the book
 does not treat and which is solved here by the divide et impera of chapter 12,
 by a sweep and by a randomized grid. This page collects what the headers take
 for granted: the notation and the predicate of orientation on which every
 algorithm rests (see \ref theory_geometry_notation), and, for each problem,
 the property on which the correctness of its algorithms rests, the argument
 behind their bounds and the lower bound that they meet (see \ref
 theory_geometry_hull and \ref theory_geometry_closest). The pseudocode of each
 algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its
 function, together with the section of the book, or the paper, that it
 follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_geometry_notation Definitions and notation

 A point \f$ p = ( x_p , y_p ) \f$ has integer coordinates (`Point` of
 `ConvexHull.h`), and the input is a set \f$ P \f$ of \f$ n \f$ points,
 possibly repeated. A set of the plane is <em>convex</em> if it contains the
 segment between any two of its points, and the <em>convex hull</em> of
 \f$ P \f$ is the smallest convex set that contains it, a polygon whose
 <em>vertices</em> are points of \f$ P \f$; the headers give the vertices in
 counterclockwise order from the lowest point (the rightmost of the lowest
 ones), with no vertex strictly inside an edge, so that the hull is unique.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{cross}( a , b , c ) \f$</td>
     <td>the cross product \f$ ( b - a ) \times ( c - a ) \f$, positive if
         \f$ c \f$ is to the left of the line from \f$ a \f$ to \f$ b \f$,
         negative if to the right, 0 if on it (<tt>cross()</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ d( p , q ) \f$, \f$ d^2( p , q ) \f$</td>
     <td>the Euclidean distance of two points and its square
         (<tt>squaredDistance()</tt>)</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \delta \f$</td>
     <td>the least distance of two points found so far by an algorithm of
         the closest pair</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$, \f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the number of vertices of the hull, and the number of points that
         survive a filter before the hull is built</td></tr>
 </table>

 The orientation of three points is the sign of
 \f[
   \mathrm{cross}( a , b , c ) = ( x_b - x_a ) ( y_c - y_a )
                               - ( y_b - y_a ) ( x_c - x_a ) , \tag{1}
 \f]
 i.e., twice the signed area of the triangle \f$ a b c \f$, which is positive
 when the turn from \f$ a \f$ through \f$ b \f$ to \f$ c \f$ is to the left.
 On integer coordinates (1) is exact, which makes every decision of the
 algorithms below exact too: with \f$ |x| , |y| \le 10^9 \f$ each factor of
 (1) is below \f$ 2^{31} \f$ in absolute value and the products fit a
 `long long`, and the same bound keeps the square of a distance within 63
 bits. Two points \f$ p \f$ and \f$ q \f$ are on the same side of the line
 through \f$ p_1 \f$ and \f$ p_2 \f$ (`sameSide()`) when the two signs of (1)
 agree, and the angle of a vector is compared without trigonometry either by
 (1) or by `pseudoAngle()`, an increasing function of the angle with values
 in \f$ [ 0 , 4 ) \f$ (exercise 16.1).

 \section theory_geometry_hull The convex hull

 The algorithms of `ConvexHull.h` rest on one characterization of the edges,
 which is the following.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the edges of the hull).</em> Two points \f$ p , q \in P \f$ are
 consecutive vertices of the hull in counterclockwise order if and only if
 \f[
   \mathrm{cross}( p , q , r ) \ge 0 \quad \forall r \in P , \tag{2}
 \f]
 and no point of \f$ P \f$ lies strictly inside the segment \f$ p q \f$.

 Indeed, the line through \f$ p \f$ and \f$ q \f$ leaves all of \f$ P \f$, and
 hence its hull, in the closed half-plane on its left, so that the segment
 \f$ p q \f$ is on the boundary of the hull; the second condition makes its
 ends vertices rather than inner points of a longer edge. Checking (2) for each
 of the \f$ n ( n - 1 ) \f$ ordered pairs costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ each, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n^3 ) \f$ in all (`convexHullBrute()`).

 The scan of Graham (`graham()`) avoids the pairs that cannot be edges. The
 lowest point \f$ o \f$ is a vertex, and the other points sorted by angle
 around it are scanned with a stack \f$ S \f$ that keeps the following
 invariant: after the scan of the \f$ i \f$-th point \f$ p_i \f$,
 \f[
   S \text{ holds, from its bottom, the vertices of the hull of }
   \{ o , p_1 , \ldots , p_i \} \text{ in counterclockwise order} . \tag{3}
 \f]
 A new point \f$ p_{i+1} \f$ is a vertex of the next hull, since it has the
 largest angle; a vertex \f$ t \f$ on top of \f$ S \f$, below which lies
 \f$ s \f$, stays a vertex if and only if the turn from \f$ s \f$ through
 \f$ t \f$ to \f$ p_{i+1} \f$ is to the left, since otherwise \f$ t \f$ lies in
 the triangle \f$ o s p_{i+1} \f$. Popping the vertices that make a turn not
 to the left, a backtrack on the choices made, restores (3). Each point is
 pushed once and popped at most once; hence the scan costs \f$ O( n ) \f$, and
 the sort by angle, \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$, dominates. Of the points collinear
 with \f$ o \f$ only the farthest is kept, and on a tie of the angle the nearer
 point comes first, so that no vertex is left strictly inside an edge.

 The bound is the best possible for the algorithms that compare: the points
 \f$ ( a_i , a_i^2 ) \f$ lie on a parabola, each one is a vertex of their
 hull, and the hull gives them in the order of the \f$ a_i \f$; hence a hull
 in \f$ o( n \log n ) \f$ would sort \f$ n \f$ numbers in
 \f$ o( n \log n ) \f$ on the algebraic decision trees, where sorting costs
 \f$ \Omega( n \log n ) \f$.

 The lower bound concerns the worst case, and `convexHull()` is faster on the
 usual inputs by two devices. First, the points extreme in the eight
 directions of the axes and of the diagonals are vertices, and a point
 strictly inside their octagon is not; the heuristic of Akl and Toussaint
 drops it with at most 8 evaluations of (1), and for points uniform in a
 square or in a disk the octagon contains all of them but a vanishing
 fraction, so that \f$ k = o( n ) \f$ points survive in expectation (Devroye
 and Toussaint). Second, the survivors are sorted by \f$ x \f$ and then
 \f$ y \f$ instead of by angle, which allows a radix sort of keys of 64 bits
 in \f$ O( k ) \f$; the monotone chain of Andrew then builds the lower and the
 upper chain of the hull by the scan of Graham from the leftmost point to the
 rightmost and back, with the same invariant (3) on each chain.

 <table>
 <caption>The algorithms of the convex hull</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Time</th><th>Source</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>convexHullBrute()</tt></td><td>every segment checked by
     (2)</td><td>\f$ O( n^3 ) \f$</td><td>section 16.1</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>graham()</tt></td><td>sort by angle and scan with a
     stack</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td><td>Graham (1972),
     section 16.1</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>convexHull()</tt></td><td>octagon filter, radix sort,
     monotone chain</td><td>\f$ O( n + k ) \f$ after a sort of \f$ k \f$
     keys in \f$ O( k ) \f$; \f$ O( n ) \f$ expected on uniform
     points</td><td>Akl and Toussaint (1978), Andrew (1979)</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_geometry_closest The closest pair

 The closest pair of \f$ P \f$, \f$ n \ge 2 \f$, minimizes \f$ d( p , q ) \f$
 over the pairs of distinct places, and the comparison of all the pairs costs
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$ (`closestPairBrute()`). The headers compare the squares
 \f$ d^2 \f$, which are integers, so that no root is ever taken.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the strip).</em> Let the points be split by a vertical line
 \f$ x = x_m \f$ into two halves whose closest pairs are at distance at least
 \f$ \delta \f$. A pair closer than \f$ \delta \f$ that crosses the line has
 both points in the strip \f$ | x - x_m | < \delta \f$, and in the strip sorted
 by \f$ y \f$ each point has at most 7 others within \f$ \delta \f$ above
 it.

 Indeed, the points of the strip with ordinate in \f$ [ y , y + \delta ) \f$
 lie in a \f$ 2 \delta \times \delta \f$ rectangle whose two
 \f$ \delta \times \delta \f$ squares, one on each side of the line, hold at
 most 4 points each, since the four \f$ \delta / 2 \f$ squares of each hold at
 most one point of its half (their diameter is below \f$ \delta \f$).

 The divide et impera of `closestPair()` sorts the points by \f$ x \f$ once,
 solves the two halves recursively, and scans the strip comparing each point
 with the next ones until their difference in \f$ y \f$ reaches
 \f$ \delta \f$, i.e., with \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ of them by Lemma 2. The strip is
 sorted by \f$ y \f$ in linear time since each recursive call returns its range
 sorted by \f$ y \f$, and the two ranges are merged as in mergesort; hence
 \f[
   T( n ) = 2 T( n / 2 ) + O( n ) = O( n \log n ) . \tag{4}
 \f]
 This is optimal on the algebraic decision trees, since \f$ n \f$ numbers are
 distinct if and only if the closest pair of the points
 \f$ ( a_i , 0 ) \f$ is at a positive distance, and the distinctness of
 \f$ n \f$ numbers costs \f$ \Omega( n \log n ) \f$ there.

 The randomized algorithms escape the lower bound by the floor function,
 which the decision trees do not have. In the grid of Golin, Raman, Schwarz
 and Smid the points are taken in a random order, and the \f$ i \f$-th one is
 compared with the points of the 9 cells around its own in a grid of side
 \f$ \lceil \delta_i \rceil \f$, \f$ \delta_i \f$ the least distance among the
 points before it; a cell holds \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ of those points, since they are
 at distance at least \f$ \delta_i \f$ from one another. The grid is rebuilt,
 in \f$ O( i ) \f$, only when the \f$ i \f$-th point lowers the least distance,
 i.e., when it belongs to the closest pair of the first \f$ i \f$ points; over
 the random order this happens with probability at most \f$ 2 / i \f$, since
 that pair has two points and each of the \f$ i \f$ is the last with the same
 probability (a backward analysis). The expected time is therefore
 \f[
   \sum_{i=2}^{n} \Bigl( O( 1 ) + \frac{2}{i} \, O( i ) \Bigr) = O( n ) ,
   \tag{5}
 \f]
 on any input, the expectation being over the random order only.

 The sweep of `closestPairSweep()` exploits the usual inputs instead: the
 points sorted by \f$ x \f$ (by radix, in \f$ O( n ) \f$) are scanned from the
 left, and each one is compared with the ones before it whose difference in
 \f$ x \f$ is below the current \f$ \delta \f$. On points spread in the plane
 there are \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ of them, and the sweep is linear; on inputs that
 crowd the strip with points far apart in \f$ y \f$ it is not, and after
 \f$ 16 n \f$ comparisons the sweep gives up, tries the other axis and, if
 that fails too, leaves the problem to the grid, whose bound (5) holds on any
 input.

 <table>
 <caption>The algorithms of the closest pair</caption>
 <tr><th>Function</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>closestPairBrute()</tt></td><td>all the pairs</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>closestPair()</tt></td><td>divide et impera, Shamos and
     Hoey</td><td>\f$ O( n \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>closestPairSweep()</tt></td><td>sweep on a radix sort, then
     the randomized grid</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ when the sweep succeeds,
     \f$ O( n ) \f$ expected always</td></tr>
 </table>

